1
00:00:04,974 --> 00:00:10,788
Hi everyone, I'm Ashley Foss, head of lifecycle marketing here at Atlassian, and I am so excited to welcome you today.

2
00:00:11,502 --> 00:00:13,508
Everyone is talking about AI.

3
00:00:13,934 --> 00:00:21,124
But here's the thing: there are a thousand AI tools that can write your emails, summarize your meetings, and draft your docs.

4
00:00:21,838 --> 00:00:31,332
They're impressive in a demo, but the moment you bring them into your actual work, your sprint reviews, your reporting workflows, your service desk, something breaks down.

5
00:00:32,078 --> 00:00:35,236
The AI doesn't know your projects, your team, or your data.

6
00:00:35,598 --> 00:00:37,956
So the output isn't quite right.

7
00:00:38,638 --> 00:00:44,996
That's the gap we're going to close today with customers who are using Atlassian's AI, Rovo, in their team workflows.

8
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:53,796
We'll hear what challenges their team was facing, the Rovo agents and workflows they built to solve those challenges, and how their teams have benefited as a result.

9
00:00:54,446 --> 00:00:58,468
By the end, I think you'll be thinking about what you want to build for your own team.

10
00:00:59,182 --> 00:01:00,996
So let's meet our customers.

11
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:11,204
We'll hear from Flow Health, a health and wellness company whose product teams automated reporting across 40 teams and three countries, and Expedia Group.

12
00:01:11,726 --> 00:01:23,844
Their 17,000-person travel company whose platform and support teams built enterprise agents to triage and content hygiene, now running over 1,200 agents in their org.

13
00:01:24,718 --> 00:01:25,924
So let's begin.

14
00:01:27,406 --> 00:01:37,188
Joining us today is Vitali Kakarczyk, head of product operations at Flow Health, the world's leading women's health platform with over 70 million monthly active users.

15
00:01:37,582 --> 00:01:43,428
Over the past six years, Vitali built and shaped Flow's product operation functions from the ground up.

16
00:01:44,174 --> 00:01:58,560
He started as an agile coach, and now product operations is a leadership discipline at Flow, setting the direction model, owning planning and prioritization, and creating the structures that allow product teams to operate with clarity and accountability at scale.

17
00:01:58,894 --> 00:01:59,760
Welcome, Vitali.

18
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:00,996
Glad to be here.

19
00:02:01,550 --> 00:02:02,000
Hello.

20
00:02:02,446 --> 00:02:03,492
Good to have you.

21
00:02:03,918 --> 00:02:06,960
So you run product operations for over 40 teams.

22
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:15,440
And before we get into a demo of how you're using Rovo, can you tell me a little bit about the challenge that your product team was facing that actually led you to use Rovo?

23
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,940
Yeah, first of all, probably a small introduction about our company.

24
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:25,040
Flow Health is their world's number one women health application.

25
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:28,932
We have more than 78 million monthly active users.

26
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:32,960
We have 380 million downloads.

27
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:38,948
And right now we are more than 350 people in product and engineering alone.

28
00:02:39,822 --> 00:02:45,220
And my team, actually, product operation team, it sits at the center of the organization.

29
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:57,156
And to make the description of a team as short as possible, we are responsible for making sure that everything is visible, coordinated, and moving.

30
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:03,200
And we do not have 40 teams yet, but we are like approaching these numbers.

31
00:03:03,438 --> 00:03:16,960
But I can say that with the 20 plus cross-functional product teams and the product surface that spans mobile, AI, content, medical, commercial.

32
00:03:17,294 --> 00:03:26,660
So we are not talking only about like typical product team where you have engineers and product manager, but for us it's like expanded team.

33
00:03:27,182 --> 00:03:30,820
The coordination of all of these teams is really a challenge.

34
00:03:31,150 --> 00:03:45,124
And at any given time, we have multiple large strategic initiatives running in parallel, and each one touching like six, eight, sometimes even ten teams simultaneously.

35
00:03:46,446 --> 00:03:53,284
Specific friction that led us to Rover was our fortnightly roadmap status meeting.

36
00:03:53,774 --> 00:04:05,184
You know that when you are scaling as a company, growing as a company, you have much more meetings where you need to update people about different statuses.

37
00:04:05,424 --> 00:04:09,732
So on a team level, be weekly, you have one level of information.

38
00:04:10,064 --> 00:04:14,624
On monthly business review meetings, you have another people and another level of information.

39
00:04:14,704 --> 00:04:21,796
And going up, you have to be as short as possible and as complete as possible, as concrete as possible.

40
00:04:22,734 --> 00:04:42,884
So, and what I realized, so one day, having more than 60 people in one room, having 20 presenters, and each team getting about three minutes to share their update, each presenter would open their Jira ticket, live on the call and read out the latest comment.

41
00:04:43,344 --> 00:04:44,612
every single time.

42
00:04:45,104 --> 00:04:53,316
And when you multiply that across 20 teams, the meeting becomes a Jira navigation exercise rather than a strategic conversation.

43
00:04:53,904 --> 00:04:58,276
And of course, all the status updates were hated by people.

44
00:04:58,864 --> 00:05:16,388
And outside of this meeting, every sprint, I need also to put the progress summary for the leadership, which meant going into the Jira manually, reading through initiatives after the initiative, and trying to place together a coherent picture.

45
00:05:17,024 --> 00:05:18,944
And data was all there.

46
00:05:19,886 --> 00:05:27,620
It just lived in 40 different places and required a human to collect, to go and collect every single time.

47
00:05:28,334 --> 00:05:43,040
I'm feeling very stressed at like a little PTSD from past lives where you're duplicating, you're literally manually going in and copying and pasting things and then same thing, the call out about just status updates versus a strategic conversation.

48
00:05:43,758 --> 00:05:45,584
All of this, I feel very stressed for you.

49
00:05:45,664 --> 00:05:48,704
So now I'm very excited that you know you're past those days.

50
00:05:48,784 --> 00:05:51,024
You've actually used Robo to help you solve this.

51
00:05:51,104 --> 00:05:58,144
So talk to me a little bit about what you built, because again, I feel like probably most of our listeners are probably like very stressed about this.

52
00:05:58,224 --> 00:05:59,588
So tell me how you solved it.

53
00:06:00,464 --> 00:06:18,624
Yeah, I was stressed as well because when you need to present, like you have very less amount of time to present everything, what you want to talk, and you know that what exactly is necessary to discuss on this meeting is blockers, for example, or risks.

54
00:06:18,784 --> 00:06:21,264
You do not need to do status reports.

55
00:06:21,424 --> 00:06:29,504
And you know, uh, be honest with you, we didn't have a data problem, so it was more an assembly problem.

56
00:06:29,806 --> 00:06:35,876
So what we built with Rover is an agent that does the assembly automatically.

57
00:06:36,846 --> 00:06:38,692
Here is how it works in practice.

58
00:06:39,438 --> 00:06:46,628
Before every status meeting, the agents queries all active initiatives from the roadmap.

59
00:06:47,726 --> 00:06:58,624
We use the projects in Jira or spaces in Jira, and it pulls the latest sprint update commitment from each initiative, parses it.

60
00:06:59,150 --> 00:07:08,292
so checking all the checkboxes, links, mentions, and groups, everything by team in our standard presenter order.

61
00:07:09,024 --> 00:07:16,708
It outputs a formatted dashboard that goes out to all attendees before the meeting starts.

62
00:07:17,184 --> 00:07:21,124
And the whole thing runs in under a minute.

63
00:07:22,222 --> 00:07:29,508
Presenters update the Jira ticket by midday on meeting day, and the dashboard is ready right before the call.

64
00:07:29,824 --> 00:07:33,540
So nobody opens Jira during their meeting anymore.

65
00:07:34,144 --> 00:07:38,148
But We took it one step further.

66
00:07:38,542 --> 00:07:41,828
After a meeting, a second workflow kicks in.

67
00:07:42,128 --> 00:07:52,132
The agent takes the transcript of the call, matches each discussions point to the right initiative, and then auto-populates the meeting notes per team.

68
00:07:52,622 --> 00:08:03,588
So instead of having someone who spent two hours writing up the summary, who said what, what action items we had, they just reviewed the draft.

69
00:08:04,048 --> 00:08:14,180
And that's the moment it stopped feeling like a time saver and started feeling like a genuine shift in how we work.

70
00:08:14,638 --> 00:08:17,168
So, Vitali, my stress level is coming down, right?

71
00:08:17,488 --> 00:08:21,408
I'm glad we're not in two hours of meetings that are just status updates.

72
00:08:21,550 --> 00:08:26,688
It sounds like this has had a huge impact on the team and it's had significant benefits.

73
00:08:26,848 --> 00:08:31,248
So tell me a little bit about what the benefits have been and how you're working now.

74
00:08:31,648 --> 00:08:34,340
Yeah, first of all, the most immediate thing is time.

75
00:08:34,607 --> 00:08:40,932
What we used to be a few hours of preparation work before every status cycle now takes one prompt.

76
00:08:41,518 --> 00:08:48,448
I will be honest with you that the prompt was not perfect from the first time.

77
00:08:48,686 --> 00:08:57,808
So we need a couple of iterations to adjust it, and we need to verify this prompt and actually result with different people who will consume this information.

78
00:08:58,222 --> 00:09:02,180
But the more meaningful change is qualitative.

79
00:09:02,574 --> 00:09:10,084
So when people arrive at the meeting and the information is already in front of them, the conversation actually changed.

80
00:09:10,848 --> 00:09:20,128
You skip the first two minutes of every update here, what we did last sprint, here what we will be doing next sprint.

81
00:09:20,334 --> 00:09:25,668
So it goes straight to what's blocking us and what decision we will need to take.

82
00:09:26,608 --> 00:09:29,444
and this is the fundamentally different meeting.

83
00:09:29,902 --> 00:09:43,808
And for my team, especially, the product operations we embedded in each product vertical, it freed up the kind of focus time that we use to get absorbed by a coordination overhead.

84
00:09:44,046 --> 00:09:54,500
So that's the time we let them work proactively, flag risks earlier, and actually influence decision rather than just track them.

85
00:09:55,374 --> 00:09:59,008
And there is something harder to measure, but important.

86
00:09:59,648 --> 00:10:11,248
People trust the information more because it's coming directly from Jira, not from someone's interpretation of Jira or someone's view, how it looks like.

87
00:10:11,568 --> 00:10:11,808
Yeah.

88
00:10:12,206 --> 00:10:16,448
There's so many things in there around where the human judgment comes in.

89
00:10:16,622 --> 00:10:22,308
Because as you note, when everybody's just kind of firing things off verbally, it's really hard to process and trust it.

90
00:10:22,528 --> 00:10:31,328
But then you don't actually get to the stuff that the humans really do need to weigh in on, like blockers, like risks, and how to actually remove blockers and solve risks, right?

91
00:10:31,598 --> 00:10:39,568
The factual information that Robo can help you generate from a ticket, that doesn't actually it can't give you the judgment in terms of the risk and the blocker.

92
00:10:39,728 --> 00:10:51,168
So I love that you've freed up the people from a processing perspective, both on not wasting time just getting facts, but also having more time to really do the things that humans are uniquely qualified to do.

93
00:10:51,328 --> 00:10:53,008
So that's amazing progress.

94
00:10:53,168 --> 00:10:54,368
I love to hear that.

95
00:10:54,528 --> 00:11:04,608
Um, for folks who are just looking to get started on this and they're thinking about running their own type of initiative or bringing Rovo into their workflows, what's some advice that you can give them to help them get started?

96
00:11:04,928 --> 00:11:08,208
I have three, even more than one.

97
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:15,328
Probably they will be trivial, but uh I really followed all of them and I suggest uh to do the same.

98
00:11:15,488 --> 00:11:21,700
So, first of all, the first one will be start from the meeting you hate most.

99
00:11:22,382 --> 00:11:36,036
Not the biggest problem, probably it will be, not probably the most ambitious automation, but just the recurring moment where you are sitting thinking, this is should not be for human.

100
00:11:36,174 --> 00:11:38,368
So this is not require a human.

101
00:11:38,688 --> 00:11:45,764
That constraint forces you to be specific enough that the agent actually has something useful to do.

102
00:11:46,208 --> 00:11:56,196
And for us, it was status meetings, so definitely we can use people's brains more effectively rather than collecting information from different gear statuses.

103
00:11:57,294 --> 00:12:01,700
Second, your data structure matters more than your agent.

104
00:12:02,448 --> 00:12:13,476
I'm going back to the prompt, I mentioned that we had a couple of iterations and rovo, and it doesn't actually matter which agent you will use.

105
00:12:14,670 --> 00:12:18,736
Rovo is good when you have a good information in Jira.

106
00:12:19,136 --> 00:12:24,708
And before we build the agent, teams needed to be updating their initiatives consistently.

107
00:12:25,134 --> 00:12:39,204
And the automation actually made the discipline visible because suddenly, if your Jira commitment was missing for two months or was the two months old, everyone now started to see it.

108
00:12:39,790 --> 00:12:41,696
And this is a useful side effect.

109
00:12:41,776 --> 00:12:53,220
So you cannot skip, you cannot say that for some reason I did not put this information into Jira because, like everyone see right now this side effect.

110
00:12:53,966 --> 00:12:57,892
And a third one, don't try to solve everything at once.

111
00:12:58,510 --> 00:13:00,176
Probably this is the most important one.

112
00:13:00,336 --> 00:13:04,644
So we started from one agent for one meeting.

113
00:13:05,136 --> 00:13:09,572
Once the team saw it working, the ideas came naturally.

114
00:13:10,414 --> 00:13:17,696
It is the way how you sell actually your automation to your stakeholders when you need to explain why we need this automation.

115
00:13:18,256 --> 00:13:30,820
And now we have agents helping us with release planning, with initiative tracking, with creative studio workflows, but it started with one small problem which was solved properly.

116
00:13:31,056 --> 00:13:33,456
So people started to use the result.

117
00:13:33,936 --> 00:13:37,216
And the hardest part isn't the technology, definitely.

118
00:13:37,376 --> 00:13:41,316
It's the getting specific enough about the problem first.

119
00:13:41,776 --> 00:13:42,256
I love that.

120
00:13:42,416 --> 00:13:47,696
And I love the, again, that human element and the process element and communication element.

121
00:13:48,014 --> 00:13:54,532
It's not just defining the problem, it's okay, do you have all of the underlying pieces to actually lay a proper foundation?

122
00:13:54,976 --> 00:14:04,576
if you keep trying to solve everything faster and faster and faster, but you haven't actually slowed down enough to make the problem specific and set the foundations, you're just solving the wrong thing faster.

123
00:14:04,736 --> 00:14:08,416
So I love, how all of those three tips work together.

124
00:14:08,496 --> 00:14:09,296
That's amazing.

125
00:14:09,456 --> 00:14:12,096
So thank you so much for joining us, Vitali.

126
00:14:12,494 --> 00:14:14,576
It's it's awesome to hear about what you're doing at Flow Health.

127
00:14:14,976 --> 00:14:15,296
Thank you.

128
00:14:19,246 --> 00:14:25,828
Flow Health's product teams went from manual reporting across 40 teams to automated summaries for every audience.

129
00:14:26,176 --> 00:14:29,376
Our last customer took things to an entirely different level.

130
00:14:29,774 --> 00:14:36,100
Expedia's platform and support teams now run over 1,200 agents across 17,000 employees.

131
00:14:36,814 --> 00:14:38,916
And joining us today is Himanschu Singh.

132
00:14:39,726 --> 00:14:41,188
Hi, Himanschu, good to see you.

133
00:14:42,062 --> 00:14:42,816
Hi, Ashley.

134
00:14:42,896 --> 00:14:43,748
Glad to be here.

135
00:14:44,256 --> 00:14:50,816
Yeah, so when you rolled out Rovo, you didn't just pick a use case, you actually crowdsourced the problem.

136
00:14:50,976 --> 00:14:53,124
So tell me a little bit about how you did that.

137
00:14:53,856 --> 00:14:55,012
great question, Ashley.

138
00:14:55,790 --> 00:15:00,816
and honestly, this is where a lot of teams get it wrong, right?

139
00:15:00,896 --> 00:15:04,416
They start with the technology, we started with the people, right?

140
00:15:04,496 --> 00:15:14,052
So XPDI is a large, complex organization, 15,000 plus employees, and virtually every team touches Jira and Confluence and Jira service management, right?

141
00:15:14,496 --> 00:15:17,136
And in some way or other way, right?

142
00:15:17,296 --> 00:15:23,460
So when we started thinking about Robo, right, the question was wasn't what AI can do.

143
00:15:23,776 --> 00:15:26,496
It was where is the real friction, right?

144
00:15:26,798 --> 00:15:33,216
And we ran an internal survey and discovery campaign to gather use cases directly from teams, right?

145
00:15:33,486 --> 00:15:39,776
So we asked, like, what are the most uh manual, cumbersome, repetitive things you do every day, right?

146
00:15:40,174 --> 00:15:42,564
And the response was tremendous, right?

147
00:15:43,214 --> 00:15:52,356
teams across PMO, program management, marketing, technology, right, they all came back with really uh clear pain points.

148
00:15:52,590 --> 00:15:55,536
Uh two themes kept rising to the top, right?

149
00:15:55,790 --> 00:16:03,684
Buck ticket quality and triage, and where engineer was spending enormous of time on poorly written tickets, right?

150
00:16:03,950 --> 00:16:06,976
And doing a lot of back and forth for clarification.

151
00:16:07,216 --> 00:16:21,856
And then the other topic was uh confluence content decay, where right, where years of stale abandoned pages were degrading the search quality and polluting the AI context that our tools rely on, right?

152
00:16:22,126 --> 00:16:28,256
We also heard a lot of story grooming, intake quality, and release notes.

153
00:16:28,496 --> 00:16:30,788
The core insight was simple.

154
00:16:31,374 --> 00:16:34,084
Start with the pain, not the platform.

155
00:16:34,542 --> 00:16:36,740
Don't ask what Robo can do.

156
00:16:37,056 --> 00:16:41,376
Ask what is slowing your team down today, right?

157
00:16:41,486 --> 00:16:45,860
The the fo that that is the focus, what we made the difference for us.

158
00:16:46,990 --> 00:16:51,008
I love that you started with people and real problems, not with technology.

159
00:16:51,088 --> 00:16:58,208
And I think that that is something, again, just to like triple underline that, that you've got to start with the real pain, not the platform.

160
00:16:58,768 --> 00:17:00,448
that's a perfect way to describe it.

161
00:17:00,688 --> 00:17:07,568
So you got in there, you realized that everybody was wasting a bunch of time on manual processes and chasing additional information.

162
00:17:07,728 --> 00:17:12,928
So let's talk first about the bug triage agent and how you worked through that.

163
00:17:13,088 --> 00:17:18,404
Walk me through what that agent does and how it's helped your teams move faster.

164
00:17:19,246 --> 00:17:24,288
I love talking about our bug triage agent, right?

165
00:17:24,688 --> 00:17:28,367
Because the before and after is so stark.

166
00:17:28,558 --> 00:17:43,460
Before Rovo, every time a bug was filed in Jira, right, someone had to manually read it, assess the quality, and assign a priority, figure out what information is missing, and then chase the reporter to fill that gaps.

167
00:17:45,294 --> 00:17:46,608
It was inconsistent.

168
00:17:46,798 --> 00:17:49,568
It was inconsistent and it was slow.

169
00:17:49,808 --> 00:17:55,140
It was consuming hundreds of hours of engineering time every single month.

170
00:17:55,502 --> 00:18:00,228
So engineers were doing admin work instead of actually building things.

171
00:18:00,928 --> 00:18:01,316
Right.

172
00:18:01,902 --> 00:18:03,940
So that's the that's the real cost.

173
00:18:04,398 --> 00:18:08,804
So we built a rovo agent that sits right inside the workflow.

174
00:18:09,328 --> 00:18:22,724
Now the moment a buck ticket gets created in Jira, the agent automatically reads it, assess the quality, assign the suggested priority, and identifies exactly what's missing.

175
00:18:23,408 --> 00:18:23,780
Right.

176
00:18:24,558 --> 00:18:30,916
Things like steps to reproduce, environment details, expected words versus actual behavior.

177
00:18:31,918 --> 00:18:41,408
Then it sends a structured Slack message directly to the reporter with a specific actionable guidance that this piece is missing in this ticket, right?

178
00:18:41,838 --> 00:18:43,684
The impact has been significant.

179
00:18:43,968 --> 00:18:50,820
We are, I mean, we are saving more than 300 hours per month just by this robo agent.

180
00:18:51,886 --> 00:18:52,448
That's crazy.

181
00:18:52,528 --> 00:18:55,088
I just, and I love that it's happening where work happens.

182
00:18:55,168 --> 00:18:59,684
I think this is another good point to call out that you know people need a ping in Slack.

183
00:18:59,888 --> 00:19:02,608
That's where they're spending a lot of time, that's where they need to see things.

184
00:19:02,688 --> 00:19:04,928
And it's not just, oh, go to the ticket for context.

185
00:19:05,088 --> 00:19:06,528
It's literally here is the next step.

186
00:19:06,688 --> 00:19:14,368
So it's a smart agent where someone works, not just, hey, by the way, something happened and fear not, I got you a little extra information.

187
00:19:14,528 --> 00:19:21,060
So I love that you guys have taken this step further to literally put the actionable steps where somebody is working.

188
00:19:21,902 --> 00:19:22,448
And absolutely.

189
00:19:22,528 --> 00:19:27,648
And this is not one agent, it's uh multiple agents doing this thing together, right?

190
00:19:27,950 --> 00:19:30,448
So think of a army of agents, right?

191
00:19:30,528 --> 00:19:33,668
Where you have a master agent and then you have subagents, right?

192
00:19:33,966 --> 00:19:53,728
And then sub agents is doing some, you know, one of the sub-agent is doing scanning the ticket, identifying the gaps, other agent is picking those gaps, making those suggestions directly to the reporter through a Slack message that yo, this is the you know, gaps that we found and fill that gap, right?

193
00:19:53,888 --> 00:19:54,368
So

194
00:19:54,368 --> 00:19:55,408
yeah, that's great.

195
00:19:55,488 --> 00:19:57,328
And and the time savings is amazing.

196
00:19:57,568 --> 00:20:07,168
So that's one piece where you kind of improve the flow and you've got the orchestration of the agents to help people save time by having more context right where they work.

197
00:20:07,328 --> 00:20:19,088
Let's switch gears a little bit and talk to me about the confluence lifecycle agent because this is a huge problem in a large organization where everyone's creating documentation, they've got their notes, they've got their project tickets, like it's a lot.

198
00:20:19,248 --> 00:20:24,324
So, how are y'all thinking about that decay and the search quality with this agent?

199
00:20:25,230 --> 00:20:29,680
